1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:01,872
The last day of October.

2
00:00:01,922 --> 00:00:07,344
Malika's accountant puts the bank's payouts next to the shop's own orders.

3
00:00:07,394 --> 00:00:12,384
One week is off by the price of two bouquets, and nobody can say why.

4
00:00:12,434 --> 00:00:15,024
Was last week's refund counted today?

5
00:00:15,074 --> 00:00:17,832
Was a reversed payment counted as a sale?

6
00:00:17,882 --> 00:00:20,376
Did one night simply not run?

7
00:00:20,426 --> 00:00:24,216
A total that nobody can take apart cannot be trusted.

8
00:00:24,266 --> 00:00:30,816
In Lesson 1, ecom counted at the end of each day what the shop earned, and kept the bank's fee.

9
00:00:30,866 --> 00:00:36,672
This lesson builds that count, night by night, so every payout can be explained.

10
00:00:36,722 --> 00:00:41,112
Every night, the bank counts the day for each website.

11
00:00:41,162 --> 00:00:46,896
Two things make that count trustworthy: a clear day, and a count you can repeat.

12
00:00:46,946 --> 00:00:48,888
First, the clear day.

13
00:00:48,938 --> 00:00:54,360
A business day runs from midnight to midnight, in the bank's own time zone.

14
00:00:54,410 --> 00:00:59,880
It is the same day that decides whether money goes back by reversal or by refund.

15
00:00:59,930 --> 00:01:06,576
So the reports and the refund rules never disagree about which day a payment belongs to.

16
00:01:06,626 --> 00:01:08,448
Then the count.

17
00:01:08,498 --> 00:01:13,464
Every night, the bank adds up each website's day in four numbers.

18
00:01:13,514 --> 00:01:17,640
Paid is the payments made that day that were not reversed.

19
00:01:17,690 --> 00:01:23,184
Refunded is the refunds made that day, whenever the original payment was.

20
00:01:23,234 --> 00:01:26,928
The fee is the bank's share, from the bank's fee rules.

21
00:01:26,978 --> 00:01:34,632
And the payout is what the merchant receives: what was paid, less what was refunded, each after its fee.

22
00:01:34,682 --> 00:01:39,720
A day with many refunds and few sales gives a negative payout.

23
00:01:39,770 --> 00:01:41,592
That is not an error.

24
00:01:41,642 --> 00:01:43,992
It is the honest answer for that day.

25
00:01:44,042 --> 00:01:45,864
Now the part you can repeat.

26
00:01:45,914 --> 00:01:50,720
What should happen when a night is counted again?

27
00:01:50,770 --> 00:01:53,288
Counting a day again is a rebuild.

28
00:01:53,338 --> 00:01:57,656
A rebuild replaces the day's numbers; it never adds to them.

29
00:01:57,706 --> 00:02:00,320
This is where I see most teams go wrong.

30
00:02:00,370 --> 00:02:08,024
They add each night's numbers to a running total, and the first time someone reruns a night, the merchant is paid twice.

31
00:02:08,074 --> 00:02:14,144
So each night, the bank counts not only yesterday, but a few days before it again.

32
00:02:14,194 --> 00:02:18,584
If one night is missed, the next night simply catches up.

33
00:02:18,634 --> 00:02:21,056
Nothing is ever counted twice.

34
00:02:21,106 --> 00:02:30,056
And behind every total sits a list of every line: each payment, and each refund as a negative line.

35
00:02:30,106 --> 00:02:33,680
So any number can be explained, one line at a time.

36
00:02:33,730 --> 00:02:37,568
Malika opens her portal and chooses Reports.

37
00:02:37,618 --> 00:02:40,280
The page is called Daily reports.

38
00:02:40,330 --> 00:02:46,184
It explains that each night the bank adds up the day before for every website.

39
00:02:46,234 --> 00:02:50,264
There is one row for each website and business day.

40
00:02:50,314 --> 00:02:57,104
The columns read Business day, Paid, Bank fee, Refunded, and You receive.

41
00:02:57,154 --> 00:02:59,984
One day shows a payment and a refund.

42
00:03:00,034 --> 00:03:01,976
Paid shows the payment's amount.

43
00:03:02,026 --> 00:03:04,184
Refunded shows the refund.

44
00:03:04,234 --> 00:03:07,376
You receive shows what is left after the fee.

45
00:03:07,426 --> 00:03:13,880
On another day, refunds outweigh sales, and You receive shows a minus.

46
00:03:13,930 --> 00:03:17,960
Malika downloads that week's lines for her accountant.

47
00:03:18,010 --> 00:03:20,288
The file opens in a spreadsheet.

48
00:03:20,338 --> 00:03:27,080
The payment is there, with its order, its website, its amount, its fee and its time.

49
00:03:27,130 --> 00:03:31,088
The refund is there too, as a negative line.

50
00:03:31,138 --> 00:03:36,152
The card column shows only the first six and the last four digits.

51
00:03:36,202 --> 00:03:39,416
Add up the lines, and you get the totals on the screen.

52
00:03:39,466 --> 00:03:46,856
The accountant finds it in a minute: a refund made on Monday, for a bouquet bought the week before.

53
00:03:46,906 --> 00:03:50,696
When Malika first joined, this page was empty.

54
00:03:50,746 --> 00:03:55,520
It said a report appears the morning after the first day with payments.

55
00:03:55,570 --> 00:04:03,392
A payout the merchant can check, a total support can take apart, and no night missed or counted twice.

56
00:04:03,442 --> 00:04:06,368
That is what a clear day and a rebuild buy.

57
00:04:06,418 --> 00:04:13,544
To recap: a clear day, four numbers, and a rebuild that replaces and never adds.

58
00:04:13,594 --> 00:04:19,480
So what happens if a night is missed, or run twice?

59
00:04:19,530 --> 00:04:23,272
The next night catches up, and nothing is counted twice.

60
00:04:23,322 --> 00:04:26,848
Next: when a buyer says, I never bought this.
